<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Xiong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">274–334</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Xiong was the founding emperor of Cheng Han, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>. Son of the refugee leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-te">Li Te</a>, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in 304 and proclaimed himself emperor of Cheng in 306. He ruled until 334 with light taxes and simple laws, giving Sichuan a period of peace while northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was at war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-xiong.jpg" alt="Li Xiong" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see below · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_drs_49802792_%E7%B9%A1%E5%83%8F%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E7%BA%8C%E7%B7%A8_v.1_(%E8%9C%80%E4%B8%BB%E6%9D%8E%E9%9B%84).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Xiong, courtesy name Zhongjuan, was the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-te">Li Te</a> by his wife Lady Luo; the Book of Jin describes him as handsome, courageous and over two metres tall. His father, leader of refugees from Qin Province, made him a general in the winter of 301. In 303 the Jin governor of Yi Province, Luo Shang, broke a truce and killed Li Te, and Li Xiong's elder brother Li Dang died in battle. When his uncle Li Liu resolved to surrender, Li Xiong attacked the Jin forces without his knowledge and drove them back, and Li Liu, dying later that year, named him successor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, Li Xiong first offered the throne to the hermit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-changsheng">Fan Changsheng</a>, revered by the refugees and a supplier of food to the army; when Fan refused, Li declared himself Prince of Chengdu late in 304, a step commonly taken as the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> era. As emperor of Cheng he honoured his mother as empress dowager, occupied all of Yi Province and then largely stopped, making no serious attempt on Ning Province, though Liang Province changed hands several times.</p><p>Historians generally saw his rule as lenient and little given to interfering with the people's livelihoods. Refugees from war-torn regions settled in his peaceful realm, but he was criticised for a lack of order in government: officials drew no salaries and requisitioned supplies directly from the people, which bred corruption under his successors. He refused requests from Zhang Jun of Former Liang to give up his imperial title and become a Jin vassal, yet kept friendly relations and trade with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His wife Empress Ren had no sons, though Li Xiong had more than ten by concubines. In 324 he made his nephew Li Ban, son of Li Dang, crown prince, reasoning that the state had been founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-te">Li Te</a> and Li Dang, and overruled the objections of Li Xiang and Wang Da. In August 334 he died of an infected head wound and Li Ban succeeded him, but later that year Li Xiong's son Li Yue assassinated Li Ban and made his brother Li Qi emperor, under whom Cheng-Han began to decline.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Xiong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death his son <b>Li Xiong</b> took Chengdu in 304, styling himself King of Chengdu, and in 306 proclaimed himself emperor of Cheng.</blockquote><a href="/state/cheng-han">Cheng Han</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.66,104.06&amp;year=313" aria-label="Chengdu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7246 1045 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Li Xiong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7696 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.66,104.06&amp;year=313">What was here in 313 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
